# MCSP Propagation 0.26

Status: Constructive architecture
Qualification: Under Conditional Experiment
Repository lineage: MCSB
System: MCSP
Parent: Structural Scaffold Carrier 0.25

## 1. Purpose

MCSP SHALL have its own propagation mechanism.

Propagation carries qualified semantic pre-settlements, scaffold structure, and qualification obligations through compatible descendants without silently copying conclusions into incompatible contexts.

Propagation is semantic lineage, not mere file copying.

## 2. Propagation rule

A qualified settlement may propagate from source scaffold A to descendant scaffold B only when:

    inherited applicability remains valid;
    governing conditions remain compatible;
    no material invariant is contradicted;
    required dependencies remain qualified;
    dimension status changes are explicit;
    unresolved residue is not silently discarded.

Otherwise propagation stops at the affected boundary.

## 3. Propagation payload

A propagation may carry:

    qualified commitments
    canonical invariants
    applicability
    dimensional statuses
    orthogonal constraints
    topology obligations
    unresolved residue
    qualification state
    realization obligations
    provenance / lineage

Documentary names are not semantic payload.

## 4. Continuous pre-settlement

Propagation and continuous pre-settlement compose:

    qualified scaffold
      -> propagate inherited settlements
      -> add new qualified settlements
      -> preserve open dimensions
      -> produce successor scaffold

A successor therefore need not reconstruct every inherited semantic commitment.

## 5. Propagation modes

Provisional modes:

    INHERIT
        preserve settlement unchanged.

    REFINE
        add compatible qualified detail.

    NARROW
        reduce applicability explicitly.

    REALIZE
        fill a realization-defined dimension without redefining inherited meaning.

    PROJECT
        expose a qualified semantic view.

    SUSPEND
        stop reliance on an affected settlement pending qualification.

    REJECT
        refuse propagation because governing conditions fail.

These are scaffolding/qualification operations, not kernel primitives.

## 6. Non-propagation

The following SHALL NOT automatically propagate:

    unsupported assumptions
    failed qualification
    host-specific convenience
    assembler spelling
    incidental encoding
    undocumented side effects
    unresolved contradiction
    realization-specific behavior outside inherited applicability

## 7. Contradiction

When a descendant contradicts an inherited qualified commitment:

    stop affected propagation;
    preserve originating settlement;
    record contradiction;
    identify affected descendants;
    suspend reliance where necessary;
    successor-version the changed settlement;
    requalify affected propagation paths.

No silent mutation is permitted.

## 8. Orthogonal propagation

An OPEN orthogonal dimension remains OPEN through propagation unless a descendant explicitly settles or constrains it.

A PRESET dimension remains PRESET unless an explicit successor changes applicability and requalifies the affected semantic structure.

This protects hardware clearance.

## 9. Canonical propagation

Invoking a canonical propagates its qualified material invariant into the invoking scaffold.

Only the canonical's applicable commitments propagate.

Extensions may add commitments but SHALL NOT mutate inherited canonical meaning.

## 10. Hardware independence

Propagation supports hardware independence by allowing the same pre-settled semantic invariant to descend into materially different realization branches.

Example structural pattern:

    canonical semantic scaffold
      -> x86-64 realization branch
      -> AArch64 realization branch
      -> RISC-V realization branch
      -> DMA/device realization branch

Each branch inherits the same applicable semantic commitments while settling different realization dimensions.

Preservation is qualified per branch.

## 11. Driver propagation

A driver may inherit:

    kernel obligations
    selected canonicals
    selected extension semantics

and then settle only device-specific realization dimensions.

The driver need not inherit unrelated taxonomy or semantics.

## 12. Propagation graph

Propagation forms a directed lineage graph of qualified scaffold relations.

The graph SHALL retain:
- source;
- successor;
- propagation mode;
- inherited commitments;
- additions;
- applicability changes;
- qualification changes;
- unresolved residue.

Graph direction is lineage direction and does not define pre-semantic ORDER.

## 13. Self-propagation target

Ultimately MCSP SHALL be capable of expressing and qualifying its own propagation rules using MCSP structures.

Self-propagation does not authorize uncontrolled self-modification.

It means MCSP definitions can produce qualified successor scaffolds under their own declared propagation rules.

## 14. Kernel boundary

PROPAGATION is not added to the pre-semantic kernel.

Kernel remains:

    DETERMINATE CONDITION

Propagation begins only after semantic pre-settlements and lineage exist.

## 15. Next implementation

The structural carrier and C normalizer should include propagation tests:

    P1 renaming does not break propagation;
    P2 compatible refinement inherits qualification;
    P3 explicit narrowing changes applicability;
    P4 contradiction stops affected propagation;
    P5 OPEN remains open unless explicitly settled;
    P6 realization-defined dimensions may diverge across branches;
    P7 canonical invariant remains identical across compatible branches;
    P8 unresolved residue remains visible;
    P9 failed qualification does not propagate as PASS;
    P10 lineage is deterministically reconstructible.

No runtime PASS is asserted.
